-- Migration 35: let `updated` carry a supplied value on the synced tables.
--
-- Same shape of problem as migration 34, one layer over. These fields read:
--
--   DEFINE FIELD updated ON flashcard DEFAULT time::now() VALUE time::now();
--
-- and a field's VALUE clause is evaluated after the statement's input and
-- REPLACES it. So no write can ever set `updated` to anything other than
-- the instant of the write - including the sync importer, which needs to
-- preserve the other device's actual edit time.
--
-- Consequence on a real pull:
--
--   repo file   updated = 2026-10-14T09:00:00   (the PC's edit)
--   phone DB    updated = 2026-10-13T17:10:04   (the import moment)
--
-- Two costs:
--
--   1. Guaranteed churn. The phone's next export writes its own `updated`
--      and `written_by`, so every push/pull cycle produces two commits
--      flipping those fields back and forth. History fills with noise.
--   2. Silent loss under clock skew. Sync resolves conflicts by comparing
--      `updated`. A record imported at T looks newer than any edit made
--      before T, so a PC edit timestamped T-1h is skipped as "stale" and
--      dropped with no message. The import moment has no relationship to
--      the content's real age.
--
-- Scoped to flashcard and note deliberately - the two tables sync owns.
-- `source`, `episode`, `notebook`, `transformation`, `focus_session`,
-- `site` and `revlog` keep the existing definition. Their raw writers are
-- mostly embedding updates, and some of those callers may reasonably
-- expect `updated` to move; changing eight tables to fix two would trade a
-- known problem for an unmeasured one. `episode_profile` and
-- `speaker_profile` already declare `TYPE datetime DEFAULT time::now()`
-- with no VALUE clause, so this brings two tables in line with existing
-- practice rather than inventing a new one.
--
-- Safe to drop VALUE here because every model write supplies the field:
-- `ObjectModel.save()` sets `updated` explicitly, and so do `repo_create`
-- and `repo_update`. The only raw writer of these tables is
-- `UPDATE $record_id SET embedding = $embedding`
-- (commands/embedding_commands.py:143), which sets only the embedding -
-- and an embedding write should not move a note's content age anyway.

DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE updated ON TABLE flashcard TYPE datetime DEFAULT time::now();
DEFINE FIELD OVERWRITE updated ON TABLE note TYPE datetime DEFAULT time::now();